all right peter let's get to the pro  bowl and ordinarily i would say i don't  want to talk about the pro bowl but  here's what i'd like to talk about and i  recall  2012 the time magazine profile of roger  goodell remember the one with the  enforcer he's holding the football on  the cover in that article there was a  reference to an idea that had been  presented to him by then buccaneers head  coach greg schiano who while at rutgers  had been the coach when eric legrand  suffered a serious permanent paralysis  on a kick return shiano's idea to  replace the kickoff give the team that  would be kicking the ball off  possession of the ball fourth and 15.  you can punt you can go for it you can  fake punt you can do whatever you want  it's fourth and fifteen you decide how  to proceed and here we are over seven  years after that article came out that  concept has made its way to the pro bowl  now it's only if you are essentially  trying to simulate an on-site kick there  will be no kickoffs there will be no  punts out of fourth and 15.  you do that only if you want to try to  keep possession of the ball and peter  this is the key factor if the nfl is  ever going to get rid of the kickoff  this is the key factor you need to have  the team that is losing  you have an opportunity to try to keep  possession of the ball and this fourth  and 15 play could be the answer if they  finally decide at some point to just get  rid of the kickoff completely  i think shiano originally did this  out of basically goodwill and very very  good intentions  and mike i think it's a good idea to try  because i i do think that the kind of  head-on collisions  that are created uh in the kicking game  and look mike  one of the things that has happened you  know for all these special teams coaches  who  you know i have great respect for mike  westoff and all of these guys who over  the years have said you've got to keep  the kickoff in the game  and i i don't want to be a heretic here  but my biggest question is why  how many great plays  this year  on a kickoff were there  and how many violent collisions there  were more violent collisions on kickoff  return and coverage this year then there  were great plays and at some point you  have to ask yourself is it really worth  it to have the kick off in the game  and again i don't want to be a heretic i  don't want to ruin the game  um you know you you want to keep the  foot in football i get it  but to me  this is  the  natural progression  of everybody trying to basically say  let's make the game a little bit safer  and let's make the game a little bit  less violent  and so that's i i would definitely be in  favor of that i think it's going to be a  cool experiment we'll see where it goes  well i mean on one hand how many kick  returns do we really see anymore more  often than not especially now that the  ball is dead the moment it hits in the  end zone you just see the football hit  the ground and the guy going like this  right and it goes over his head and hits  the ground and you put the ball on the  25 anyway and the onside kick has become  almost impossible to recover so look i i  don't know what the percentages are for  converting fourth and 15 when people  know that that it's coming it's not a  fake punt but a play from scrimmage  it's got to be higher than what the  onside kick currently is because the  onside kick conversion percentage is  minuscule at this point it's so  minuscule that when the the falcons were  able to rattle off two or three of them  in a row against the saints it was one  of the stories of the year because it  just doesn't happen  and you know mike i also think this that  if you're talking about  you know john i talked to john lynch for  my podcast this week and he was talking  about how raheem mostert was such a good  special teams player  that it was like in the old days  remember when when marv levy jim kelly  was begging for steve tasker to be used  as a wide receiver and marv levy would  say i'm using them 25 28 snaps a game on  special teams i can't give them to you  you know to wear them out in the passing  game we got a good enough offense we  need to have great special teams and in  a lot of ways raheem mostert was such a  good special teams player and the 49ers  were okay at running back that they were  saying we're using we're maxing him out  on special teams and and i think the  devaluation  of the kickoff and because so many punts  are either fair caught or kicked out of  bounds  right now that in my opinion the  emphasis on special teams really has  declined so the 49ers say let's play him  in the backfield especially when we have  injuries  yeah and for so many guys performance on  special teams becomes that way that they  prove their worth and they end up  getting the attention of the offensive  or defensive coaches and then the next  thing you know they're contributors  they're just gonna have to find another  path and you know i feel badly for the  guys who make their career playing  special teams of matthew slater for  example  but  the game is changing and the game has  evolved in order to survive and they've  taken away these high impact collisions  when you have people from the league who  are saying that kickoff is the most  dangerous play in the game well you're  you're virtually guaranteeing they're  going to get rid of it at some point  it's a no-brainer it's going to be gone  but you have to have a way for a team  that is trailing to get back into the  game peter six years ago in the run-up  to the super bowl that was played in new  york roger goodell was asked by cnbc who  he roots for and he said i root for the  team that is losing they want the team  that is losing a game to have a chance  to get back into it so this fourth and  fifteen play is the way that you can  give that team a chance if you do get  rid of the kickoff  and one other thing to keep in mind mike  about this this is not the ruination of  football it's not the this it's not  making football a circus it is simply  trying to make football a safer game  what's more likely next right here  hi i'm mike tarico and thanks for  watching make sure to hit subscribe for  the latest news and highlights from nbc  sports
 

